<%#
  Stand-in for a consent banner. It only stores the visitor's choice in a cookie and
  reloads the page. On the next request the server gives the choice to the SDK
  (visitor.ts) and the page gives it to engine.js (partials/kameleoon.ejs).

  The reload keeps the example to a single path: both sides always receive the
  choice at the start of a request. On a real site the choice comes from your consent
  management platform. Call Kameleoon.API.Core.enableLegalConsent() /
  disableLegalConsent() from its callback as well, so engine.js learns the choice
  without waiting for the next page.

  Locals: consent ("granted", "denied" or "unknown")
%>
<h2>Legal consent</h2>
<p>
  Current choice: <code><%= consent %></code>.
  <% if (consent === "unknown") { %>Neither the SDK nor engine.js has been told anything yet.<% } %>
</p>
<p>
  <button type="button" onclick="storeConsent(true)">Accept</button>
  <button type="button" onclick="storeConsent(false)">Reject</button>
</p>

<script>
  function storeConsent(granted) {
    // Same cookie name as CONSENT_COOKIE in src/visitor.ts
    document.cookie = "kameleoonConsent=" + granted + "; path=/; max-age=31536000; SameSite=Lax";
    location.reload();
  }
</script>
